#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125271} :activity/id #uuid "27f87e6f-03c7-52b0-bd22-1d95ebba4299" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050164 :topic/id #uuid "32af04f7-8bd5-5a90-8e7c-6c111c983ee8" :topic/math-academy-id 1955} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125729 :tutorial/content "Just as the derivative of a function represents the slope of the tangent line to the function, the partial derivative of a function has a geometric interpretation as well.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the surface $z = f (x, y)= 18 - x^2 - y^2$ and the plane $x=3.$ The intersection of the surface and the plane forms a downward parabolic curve, as shown below. A point $P(3,2,5)$ is plotted and a tangent line is drawn through that point.\n\n![](images/86/8619c41cd0caf04ae9ee9bcae61e31739d8da583793a67c1c87e00186c3ac417.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent, we can compute the partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ and evaluate it at $P(3,2,5)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(18-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2y \\\\ {\\frac{∂f}{∂y}|}_{P} & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To verify that this result is correct, we can find the equation of the downward parabolic curve and then use single-variable calculus to compute the slope of the tangent line.\n\nTo find the equation of the downward parabolic curve, we can substitute the equation of the plane ($x=3$) into the equation of the surface ($f (x, y)= 18 - x^2 - y^2$). Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,y) & =18-{3}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\  & =9-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the downward parabolic curve is $z=g(y) = 9-y^2.$ In the $yz$-plane, this curve has the following graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e982ef5d6d8d4fec9c64957d46f22cea2a85bd002595642f58700cf48cce4256.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent to $g(y)$ at $P,$ we can use single-variable calculus. We differentiate $g(y)$ and evaluate it at $P(3,2,5).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{'}(y) & =-2y \\\\ {g}^{'}(2) & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "56aef924-0db1-5d63-a9a3-5a81bcbdc411" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2376 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef70361b-fe3c-57f6-a037-19f320382e64" :step/math-academy-id 10304 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125471}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124443 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124247 :question/id #uuid "225b0bca-3520-58ab-bd36-0e3de337b604" :question/math-academy-id "e-3068"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048713}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385493}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "f75552c6-bdfd-5938-9f73-0458e5746656" :step/math-academy-id 10305 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124248 :question/id #uuid "70bcf628-cf1f-5da0-85b7-19d0f514ccd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3069"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050641}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391983}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "2187e294-aaab-55c3-873b-46c11f1beb02" :step/math-academy-id 10312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124249 :question/id #uuid "75c0deaf-38a7-5a81-afac-cae2978d52b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3070"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050997}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385500}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "d77940fc-946a-53b1-a901-26cea615e37c" :step/math-academy-id 10315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125471 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125814 :tutorial/content "To recap, when we calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},$ we differentiate $f$ with respect to $y$ and treat $x$ as a constant. This is the same as finding the intersection of $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $x = x_0$ and calculating the slope of the resulting curve.\n\nSo, we have the following result:\n\n*Suppose that a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a plane $x = x_0$ intersect on a curve $C,$ and suppose that $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ lies on $C.$ Then $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ evaluated at $P$ gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$*\n\nWe have a similar result for the intersection of a surface and a plane $y=y_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n*Suppose that a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a plane $y = y_0$ intersect on a curve $C,$ and suppose that $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ lies on $C.$ Then $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ evaluated at $P$ gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "60c2d035-3f05-5f1d-b652-df42f673e869" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3933 :tutorial/title "The Geometric Interpretation of the Partial Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "50d32bc7-043e-58d2-a649-b9e1957fb923" :step/math-academy-id 23236 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125272}}] :activity/title "Geometric Interpretations of Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124747}]}
